it's a privilege to be online at chatham  house to follow such distinguished  lawyers philip sands depo acandy  and soon to hear mikola nanowski  and can i start by saying i'm in or of  the bravery shown by him and the foreign  minister of ukraine dimitro kulebi  and his government  courage winston churchill once said is  the greatest quality of all because upon  it all else depends  and i want also to acknowledge the  selfless courage of the people of  ukraine president putin's army may  capture ukrainian territory in people's  homes but he will never capture  ukrainian hearts and minds  already far too many lives have been  destroyed but ukraine's resolve is  indestructible hearts are being broken  every hour of the day  but the unity of ukraine is unbreakable  but the people of ukraine now need us to  confront the enormity of the challenge  that president putin's actions pose to  them and the very existence of the  post-1990 international order  he has sought to overturn the rule of  law by the use of force he seeks to  replace the principle of  self-determination with a new form of  imperialism based on a warped reading of  history in his attempt to redraw the map  of europe he seeks to re-divide east and  west  if we were to acquiesce in any way none  of us could ever take freedom for  granted ever again or be sure that  democracy or peace would triumph over  totalitarianism  for all these reasons and because of the  scale of the suffering now imposed on  the people of ukraine  i believe that most people would agree  that this act of aggression by russia  deplored in the strongest terms this  week by the un general assembly  cannot go uninvestigated unprosecuted  and unpunished  but there is as we have heard a serious  gap in international law  since russia is not a party to the  statue of the international criminal  court  then this crime of aggression cannot as  things stand be investigated by the  prosecutor  so even as we encourage carrying can qc  in the international criminal court to  investigate crimes against humanity  genocide and war crimes that may include  the targeting of civilians the use of  vacuum bonds and the threat of nuclear  terror we must also address russia's  responsibility for aggression  crimes against peace starting and  continuing the war with no ceasefire  without safe passage on humanitarian  grounds  doing so will complement the work of the  european court of human rights in  addressing violations of human rights  and the request by ukraine for the  international court of justice to  intervene on its behalf  so i'm here today to support the case my  legal friends have so eloquently set out  and the document documentation for which  i have sent to governments across europe  but most of all i'm here to support the  government of ukraine which is today  calling on the international community  to create a special tribunal to  investigate the specific crime of  aggression one for which mr putin and  his closest military political  intelligence and financial collaborators  may be found to be individually liable  having waged a war that is no  conceivable legal justification as it is  not in self-defense and is contrary to  the charge of the united nations  the special tribunal should be  constituted on the same principles that  guided what we have heard today the  allied powers in 1942 when from in  london they insisted in their statement  punishment for war crimes  that i quote war crimes should not be  left unpunished it was a statement that  led as we've heard to the international  military tribunal which led to the  trials at nuremberg  a tribunal was necessary as the 1942  declaration said to satisfy the sense of  justice of the civilized world  they wanted and i quote again to secure  the punishment through the channel of  organized justice of those guilty or  responsible for these crimes  the special tribunal for the punishment  of the crime of aggression against  ukraine can be set up with speed  and i want to call on all the countries  of europe to agree to grant jurisdiction  to the dedicated tribunal giving it the  power to investigate the principal  perpetrators of the crime of aggression  and all those who are complicit in its  commission including those who provided  finance  now the cost of war is to be counted not  only in tanks destroyed missiles fired  and bullets discharged but in the  desperate cry of wounded children the  anguish of bereaved mothers and fathers  and the tears and suffering of the  innocent  we owe it to the people of ukraine to  act now and show them that never again  will the crime of aggression go  unpunished we ought to the coming  generation to prevent the unmooring of  the international system from its  familiar anchors diplomacy the rule of  law and the willingness to work together  through international institutions  and the need is urgent as we speak  russia are surrounding ukraine's cities  tightening their grip and by their  bombing threatening to create a desert  and call it peace  yes sadly buildings are being raised to  the ground  but not the spirit of the ukrainian  people and by acting in solidarity with  them we will signal our resolve that  lawless aggression will not be tolerated  anywhere on european soil or elsewhere  and that by leaving no stun unturned and  bringing to an end this evil we ensure  that those who who've unleashed the  horrors are subject to personal  accountability under the criminal law  all those hoping for peace not only in  ukraine but across europe cry out for  justice
 

